% D-multitarget-roadmap.weft — The consolidation roadmap that must be
% completed before kcc grows a second code-generation target.
%
% The Phase 0--9 roadmap (Appendix, C-roadmap.weft) carries kcc from a
% bootable skeleton to a self-hosting, single-target production compiler.
% Its final open item (#10) is "a second target (x86-64 / ELF)."  This
% appendix is the roadmap for everything that should be finished *first*,
% organized around a single principle so the order is not arbitrary.

\chapter{Roadmap: The Road to a Second Target}
\label{ch:multitarget-roadmap}

% Status glyphs.  Defined with \providecommand so this appendix renders
% whether or not the Phase~9 roadmap (which defines the same names) has
% already been read in the same document.
\providecommand{\stDone}{\textcolor{green!55!black}{\checkmark\,\textsc{done}}}
\providecommand{\stPart}{\textcolor{orange!90!black}{$\odot$\,\textsc{partial}}}
\providecommand{\stOpen}{\textcolor{black!45}{$\circ$\,\textsc{open}}}
\providecommand{\meterA}{$\bullet\bullet\bullet$}
\providecommand{\meterB}{$\bullet\bullet\circ$}
\providecommand{\meterC}{$\bullet\circ\circ$}

\kcc{} today is a correct, self-hosting C17 compiler for one target
(ARM64 / Mach-O) that compiles and runs real software---Lua~5.4.7's
entire official suite, zlib~1.3.1 end to end, cJSON byte-identical to
\texttt{clang}.  The natural next ambition is a \emph{second} target.
This appendix argues that a second target should not be started yet, and
lays out precisely what to finish first.

% ================================================================
\section{The Guiding Principle}
\label{sec:mt-principle}

The retargeting playbook (Chapter~\ref{ch:retargeting}) establishes that
the IR is the \emph{neutral seam}: roughly seventy percent of the
compiler---the preprocessor, parser, semantic analysis, IR construction,
the optimizer, and the IR verifier---is target-independent and is reused
verbatim by every backend.  Only the last thirty percent (instruction
selection, register banks, ABI, object emission) is target-specific.

That split dictates the order of work by one rule:

\begin{center}
\fcolorbox{black!55}{black!4}{%
\parbox{0.9\linewidth}{\centering\smallskip
\textbf{Finish the shared seventy percent before you split.}\\[3pt]
Every defect in the target-neutral core that survives into a two-target
world costs \emph{twice}: it must be re-diagnosed through the ambiguity
``is this the shared frontend or the new backend?'', and every fix must
be re-validated against two code generators and two test baselines.  A
feature added to the IR optimizer \emph{before} the split is written once
and inherited free by the second target; the same feature added
\emph{after} must be proven correct on both.\smallskip}}
\end{center}

\noindent There is a second, quieter reason.  The ARM64 backend is not
just \emph{a} target---once a second target exists it becomes the
\emph{oracle}: the differential reference (\texttt{clang}-validated,
byte-identical on real code) against which the new backend's output is
checked.  An oracle must be complete and trustworthy before it is relied
upon.  So the pre-split work is of exactly two kinds: \textbf{(A)}
complete and prove the shared core, and \textbf{(B)} polish ARM64 into a
dependable oracle.

% ================================================================
\section{Milestones at a Glance}
\label{sec:mt-glance}

Five milestones (M1--M5) precede the second target (M6).  M1--M4 are
target-neutral (kind~A); M5 is the structural work that \emph{enables}
the split.  The table is the scan; the sections are the detail.

\begin{center}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@@{}clccl@@{}}
\hline
\textbf{\#} & \textbf{Milestone} & \textbf{Kind} & \textbf{Effort} & \textbf{Status}\\
\hline
M1 & Finish the C17 frontend (designators, volatile bit-fields, real \texttt{float}) & neutral & \meterB & \stDone\\
M2 & Harden the IR verifier                    & neutral & \meterB & \stDone\\
M3 & Systematic conformance battery            & neutral & \meterA & \stOpen\\
M4 & Broaden the IR optimizer                  & neutral & \meterA & \stOpen\\
M5 & Carve the target interface (route ARM64 through it) & seam & \meterA & \stOpen\\
\hline
M6 & Second target (x86-64 / ELF)              & backend & \meterA & \stOpen\\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent\emph{Effort:}\quad \meterA{}\,high \quad \meterB{}\,medium
\quad \meterC{}\,low.\quad M1--M4 may proceed in parallel; M5 must come
last of the five, because it is validated by re-running the now-complete
M1--M4 suites through the refactored ARM64 path with byte-identical
output.

% ================================================================
\section{M1 --- Finish the C17 Frontend}
\label{sec:mt-m1}

\textbf{Goal.}  Close the remaining language gaps so the shared frontend
is genuinely conforming before it feeds two backends.  Each of these is
IR-\emph{construction} work---it lives above the seam and is reused by
every target.

\textbf{Work items.}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Nested designated initializers} (\stdref{6.7.9}):
      \texttt{.a.b[2] = v} and brace-elision across aggregate levels.
      Single-level \texttt{.f=}/\texttt{[i]=} is already done; the
      recursion over sub-objects is the gap.
\item \textbf{Volatile bit-fields} \emph{(landed)}: the synthesised
      read--modify--write of a \texttt{volatile}-declared bit-field now stamps
      the volatile bit on its container load and store
      (\texttt{\_kcc\_ir\_bf\_container\_type} feeding the type-taking
      \texttt{\_kcc\_ir\_emit\_load\_t}/\texttt{\_store\_t}), so CSE and DCE
      treat each access as a mandatory side effect---parity with a volatile
      scalar member.
\item \textbf{A real single-precision \texttt{float} model} \emph{(landed)}.
      Values still live as \texttt{double} in the SIMD registers---a uniform
      backend model---but a binary operation whose \emph{result} type is
      \texttt{float} now appends an \texttt{IR\_FPROUND}, rounding that
      sub-expression to single precision as it is produced.  This gives the
      \texttt{FLT\_EVAL\_METHOD}~$0$ semantics Apple ARM64 and \texttt{clang}
      use---\texttt{a/b*c} matches \texttt{clang} to the last bit---while a
      comparison (\texttt{int} result) and a mixed \texttt{float}/%
      \texttt{double} expression (full precision) are correctly left alone.
      Settling this \emph{now} avoids re-baselining two backends later.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{weft files.}  \texttt{27b-sema-declarations} and
\texttt{32c-ir-aggregates} (designators); \texttt{32b-ir-expressions}
and \texttt{37c-codegen-dispatch} (volatile bit-fields; the value model
is neutral, the final load/store width is where ARM64 realizes it);
\texttt{28-conversions} and the IR float opcodes (\texttt{float} model).

\textbf{Acceptance.}  The differential corpus (\texttt{make difftest})
gains cases for each feature and stays byte-identical to \texttt{clang};
self-host stays byte-identical; no existing suite regresses.

\textbf{Status.}  In progress.  Nested designator chains
(\texttt{.p.a}, \texttt{.arr[1]}, \texttt{[0].x}) and brace elision
(\texttt{int m[2][2] = \{1,2,3,4\}}) are \emph{landed}, at both block and
file scope, through a single recursive initializer
(\texttt{\_kcc\_ir\_fill\_list}) shared by the local and global paths and
validated element-for-element against \texttt{clang}.  Volatile-declared bit-fields now carry the volatile bit on their
container access, at parity with volatile scalar members.  Single-precision
\texttt{float} arithmetic now rounds each \texttt{float} sub-expression once
(\texttt{FLT\_EVAL\_METHOD}~$0$), bit-exact with \texttt{clang}.  With this,
\textbf{M1 is complete}; the residual (propagating \texttt{volatile} from a
qualified \emph{object} through member access) is a general member-access
matter, not specific to this milestone.

% ================================================================
\section{M2 --- Harden the IR Verifier}
\label{sec:mt-m2}

\textbf{Goal.}  Make the IR verifier (Chapter~\ref{ch:ir-validation})
strong enough to reject malformed IR \emph{before} it reaches any
backend.  With one code generator, a backend crash and an IR defect look
alike; with two, a verifier that catches the defect at the seam is what
keeps a frontend bug from masquerading as a backend bug.

\textbf{Work items} \emph{(landed)}.  The verifier
(\texttt{\_kcc\_ir\_validate}, \S\ref{ch:ir-validation}) now walks every
function, block, and instruction and enforces three invariants whose
violation is undefined back-end behaviour: valid opcodes (an
uninitialised or corrupted \texttt{IR\_OP}---the shape of the
stale-\texttt{NOP} bug---is rejected), terminated blocks (a non-empty
block that would let code generation fall through is rejected), and finite
instruction lists (a cyclic \texttt{IR\_NEXT} is reported, not spun on).
It is wired into the driver as a mandatory pass between the optimizer and
the back end, so it also catches an optimization pass that corrupts the
IR.  Dataflow invariants that need dominance (strict def-before-use) or
that \kcc's pre-register-allocation IR does not maintain (single static
assignment) are deliberately left to a later dataflow-aware pass, to avoid
false rejections.

\textbf{weft files.}  \texttt{33-ir-validation} (real verifier replacing
the stub); \texttt{42-driver} (mandatory call before code generation).

\textbf{Acceptance.}  The verifier accepts all IR the current suites
produce---every codegen, difftest, self-host, interop, e2e case, plus all
of Lua and zlib, compiles with no false rejection---and a controlled fault
(tightening the opcode ceiling) confirms it does fire and abort the
compile with a diagnostic naming the function.

\textbf{Status.}  Done.

% ================================================================
\section{M3 --- Systematic Conformance Battery}
\label{sec:mt-m3}

\textbf{Goal.}  Prove the shared frontend and IR are correct on the
\emph{uncommon} cases that no hand-picked real program happened to
exercise---\emph{while there is still only one target}, so a failure
localizes unambiguously to the core.

\textbf{Work items.}
\begin{itemize}
\item Run an external conformance corpus (e.g.\ \texttt{c-testsuite})
      through \kcc{} and record pass/fail.
\item Add a differential harness that compiles randomly-generated
      programs (e.g.\ \texttt{csmith}) with both \kcc{} and \texttt{clang}
      and compares runtime output, shrinking any divergence to a minimal
      reproducer.
\end{itemize}
Per project policy (\S no-fuzz-harness) this uses \emph{external}
generators as oracles and fixes each surfaced bug directly; it does not
add a bespoke in-tree fuzzing suite, and the existing suites stay green.

\textbf{weft files.}  \texttt{44-test-harness} (a conformance driver
target); no compiler-source changes except the fixes each run motivates.

\textbf{Acceptance.}  A recorded baseline pass-rate on the external
corpus, and zero known-unfixed differential divergences at the chosen
generator settings.

\textbf{Status.}  Open.

% ================================================================
\section{M4 --- Broaden the IR Optimizer}
\label{sec:mt-m4}

\textbf{Goal.}  Raise generated-code quality with passes that are
\emph{entirely} IR-to-IR, so the second target inherits every one of
them at no additional cost.  This is the milestone where the ``write
once, reused free'' economics of the guiding principle pay off most.

\textbf{Work items.}  On top of the present constant folding, DCE, and
CSE: \textbf{copy propagation}, \textbf{constant propagation},
\textbf{strength reduction} (e.g.\ multiply-by-constant to shift/add),
and \textbf{inlining of small functions}.  Each runs to a fixpoint under
the existing pass manager and is verified against the ARM64 oracle.

\textbf{weft files.}  new pass fragments alongside \texttt{35-peephole},
\texttt{36-dce}, \texttt{36a-cse}; \texttt{34-pass-manager}
(sequence and fixpoint).

\textbf{Acceptance.}  Each pass preserves observable behaviour on the
full suite (self-host byte-identical is \emph{not} required here---output
is expected to improve---but runtime results and the differential corpus
must match \texttt{clang}); a size/instruction-count metric shows the
intended reduction.

\textbf{Status.}  Open.

% ================================================================
\section{M5 --- Carve the Target Interface}
\label{sec:mt-m5}

\textbf{Goal.}  Perform the structural refactor that turns ``the ARM64
backend'' into ``the first instance of a target interface,'' so a second
target slots in beside it rather than forking the backend.  This is the
step that literally \emph{enables} M6.

\textbf{Work items.}  Following the retargeting playbook
(Chapter~\ref{ch:retargeting}): express register banks as
\emph{descriptors} (so the linear-scan allocator becomes
bank-parameterized rather than ARM64-wired), lift the ABI into a
\emph{table} (argument/return classification, callee-saved sets,
alignment), and route instruction selection, prologue/epilogue, and
object emission through a \emph{target vtable}.  Add the
\texttt{-{}-target} selection plumbing to the driver.  Throughout, ARM64
remains the sole concrete target and must keep passing through the new
indirection.

\textbf{weft files.}  \texttt{37/37a/37b/37c-codegen} (behind the
vtable), \texttt{38/39-emit} and \texttt{40-linker} (object-format
parameterization), \texttt{42-driver} (\texttt{-{}-target}); the
interface itself is a new backend-infrastructure fragment.

\textbf{Acceptance.}  The \emph{whole} suite---most importantly self-host
and \texttt{difftest}---stays \textbf{byte-identical} after the refactor:
a pure structural change to ARM64's path must produce the exact same
bytes it did before.  This is why M5 comes last of the five: it is
validated by re-running the by-now-complete M1--M4 suites through the
refactored path.

\textbf{Status.}  Open.  The playbook (Chapter~\ref{ch:retargeting})
already specifies the interface shape; this milestone is its
implementation for ARM64.

% ================================================================
\section{M6 --- The Second Target (Hand-off)}
\label{sec:mt-m6}

With M1--M5 complete, adding x86-64 / Mach-O (then ELF / Linux) is the
thirty-percent problem the playbook describes: an instruction-selection
table, a register-bank descriptor, an ABI table, and an object emitter,
validated differentially against the ARM64 oracle and run under
Rosetta~/ qemu.  It is deliberately \emph{out of scope} for this
appendix, whose entire purpose is to guarantee that when M6 begins, every
failure it uncovers belongs to the new backend and nowhere else.  The
mechanics of M6 live in the retargeting playbook
(Chapter~\ref{ch:retargeting}).

% ================================================================
\section{Summary}
\label{sec:mt-summary}

The order is not a preference; it falls out of the seam.  M1--M4 finish
and prove the target-neutral seventy percent while a single target still
makes every failure unambiguous.  M5 carves the interface with ARM64 as
the only occupant, proven by byte-identical output.  Only then does M6
introduce a second occupant---by which point the shared core is complete,
the optimizer is inherited free, the verifier guards the seam, and ARM64
is a trustworthy oracle.  \emph{Finish the shared part before you split};
the rest is instruction tables.
